@nicklovescode @iamknighton I'd guess that HSL actually makes some standard low-level features harder. For example, I think you now need two layers to implement a color contrast detector since:

* Hue loops around at 360
* Very different hues can map to the same colors if saturation is low, or L is low/high

@tiagogmarques @neurograce * We can record arbitrarily many records of all neurons on any stimuli
* Weight tying means there's many fewer neurons

And possibly there's fundamentally different structure!

@tiagogmarques @neurograce Yep, there's a lot of commonalities between our study of artificial neural networks and single cell neuroscience. There's also a lot of advantages we have, though:

* We can take derivatives and optimize through the model
* We have access to not just the connectome, but weights

